    "content": [
      {
        "id": "local-arena",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A political arena beyond national parties",
        "content": "Iran’s municipal arena cannot be understood simply by transferring national factional labels onto a city map. Sahar Saeidnia’s research on Tehran describes local elections as contests over municipal resources, representation, and the meaning of public participation, alongside competition between reformist and conservative currents. Her account also identifies independent candidates as participants in this arena. It is a historical analysis of local politics, rather than a register of organizations operating today. [s1]\n\nAn electoral list is a group of candidates presented together to voters; a municipal council is the public institution to which successful candidates are elected. Keeping these categories separate helps explain why an election victory does not itself demonstrate that a durable local party organization exists. Saeidnia’s account of Tehran’s reformist victory in 2017 illustrates how negotiations among elected councilors also shaped the subsequent selection of a mayor. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "neighborhood-associations",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Neighborhood participation is a different institution",
        "content": "The neighborhood associations known as shorayari provide a useful contrast. The charter translated by Syracuse University’s Iran Data Portal explains their creation through Tehran’s city council as a response to the complexity of urban problems. Its preface argues for decentralization, consultation, and sustained participation through voluntary civic associations. This establishes the initiative’s stated rationale; it does not establish how representative or influential the associations became. [s2]\n\nThe distinction matters editorially. An association that communicates neighborhood concerns should not automatically be described as a political party, a party branch, or an electoral coalition. Conversely, a civic description alone does not answer questions about participants’ political affiliations. Those questions require separate evidence about membership, leadership, activities, and relationships with electoral groups. The charter supports an account of institutional purpose, not a current directory of local political networks. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "law-and-local-variation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Legal categories and evidence limits",
        "content": "The inspected council-law compilation contains provisions distinguishing individual candidates from electoral lists, including arrangements involving legally recognized parties and political fronts. However, its title, amendment annotations, and links to replacement versions make version control essential. It is insufficient on its own to certify which electoral arrangements applied in every locality at the research cutoff. [s3]\n\nA reliable account of a particular local organization therefore needs more than a national factional label: an identifiable organization, a dated activity record, and evidence of its local constituency. Tehran’s documented experience is a starting point, not evidence that provincial capitals, smaller towns, and villages share the same organizational patterns. This page does not identify unverified organizations as active or assign them unsupported memberships or electoral strength.",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
