    "seo": {
      "title": "Public Policy in Iran: Institutions, Priorities and Evidence",
      "description": "Explore how Iran’s public-policy objectives relate to budgets, development programs, sectoral decisions, and evidence of results."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "authority-and-budget",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From authority to a funded decision",
        "content": "Public policy concerns the choices public institutions make and the instruments used to carry them out. In Iran, the constitutional budget framework provides one concrete point of entry: the government prepares the annual budget and submits it to the Islamic Consultative Assembly, commonly called the Majles, for approval. The constitution also provides for auditing public expenditure and publishing the annual budget-settlement report. These are formal requirements, rather than findings about compliance. [s1]\n\nThis distinction guides the questions a reader should ask. A policy document can establish an objective; a budget can authorize expenditure; an implementation record can show activity. Demonstrating a beneficial result requires further evidence. None of these documents should silently substitute for the others.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "interlocking-priorities",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Policy fields overlap",
        "content": "The United Nations Sustainable Development Cooperation Framework for Iran offers a documented example of a cross-sector agenda. Announced in 2023 for the period 2023–2027, it organizes cooperation around public health, socioeconomic resilience, environmental and natural-resource management, disaster risk, and drug control. The UN described coordination with relevant ministries and alignment with national development aspirations. This is a cooperation framework, not a comprehensive statement of every Iranian public policy. [s2]\n\nIts structure helps explain why policy subjects overlap. Environmental management and disaster preparedness appear alongside health and socioeconomic resilience rather than as isolated concerns. The launch announcement explicitly distinguished signing the framework from beginning implementation. Readers should preserve that distinction when assessing subsequent claims: agreement on priorities does not by itself demonstrate completed services or improved conditions. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "plans-and-results",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading development plans critically",
        "content": "The International Energy Agency’s entry on the Seventh Development Plan Bill describes electricity-sector provisions concerning generation capacity, network losses, financing, and energy-exchange transactions. It illustrates how a development plan can combine physical infrastructure objectives with financial and market arrangements. Its numerical targets describe intended changes, not measurements of completed construction or achieved efficiency. [s3]\n\nThe entry also illustrates a source problem: it retains the word “Bill” while labeling the policy “In force.” Before relying on a particular provision as current law, an editor should reconcile the database entry with the enacted Persian text and subsequent amendments. Its reporting-period labels should likewise be checked rather than treated as interchangeable Gregorian dates. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "exploring-policy",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How to explore the subject",
        "content": "The branches below separate economic, industrial, education, environmental, technology, and regional-development policy so that each can be assessed on its own evidence. For any branch, useful questions remain consistent: What problem is defined? Which institution has responsibility? What resources are authorized? What happened in practice? Who benefited, and who remained underserved?\n\nThese are analytical questions rather than a numerical ranking of policy quality. This overview remains incomplete because the inspected material does not yet provide a balanced assessment across all six branches or establish their implementation record through the research cutoff.",
