    "seo": {
      "title": "Economic Policy in Iran: Framework, Instruments and Constraints",
      "description": "An introduction to Iran’s economic-policy framework, public spending, energy-sector planning, and the evidence needed to assess results."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "economic-framework",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The formal economic framework",
        "content": "Iran’s constitution recognizes state, cooperative, and private sectors. This is a legal framework, not a measurement of their present shares in production or ownership. The constitutional text also requires a legal basis for taxation and sets out the government’s responsibility for preparing the annual budget. These provisions establish starting points for understanding economic authority; they do not establish how every enterprise is controlled or how policy operates in practice. [s1]\n\nFor an international reader, economic policy is therefore broader than a ministry’s announced program. Its assessment needs to connect legal instruments and public spending with evidence about production, prices, employment, and household welfare. A constitutional commitment and a measured economic outcome answer different questions.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "structure-and-constraints",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A diversified economy with oil exposure",
        "content": "The World Bank describes Iran as having hydrocarbon, agricultural, and service sectors, together with a substantial state presence in manufacturing and financial services. It also identifies continuing reliance on oil revenues as a source of volatility in both economic activity and public revenue. This helps explain why oil-sector developments matter beyond the oil industry itself. [s2]\n\nThe inspected country overview identifies sanctions, conflict, and water and energy shortages as constraints on economic activity. These factors complicate attribution: a change in output cannot automatically be credited to, or blamed on, a single domestic policy. The page is mutable and does not provide a clear publication date for its current narrative, so its latest assessments require an archived pre-cutoff version before being presented as the situation on the editorial cutoff date. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "energy-policy-example",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Energy planning as an economic-policy example",
        "content": "Electricity policy demonstrates the connection between investment and the rules governing markets. The International Energy Agency’s development-plan entry describes provisions for network financing and a larger role for electricity transactions on the energy exchange. These are policy instruments: they concern how infrastructure and transactions are organized. They do not establish that investment was delivered or that shortages were resolved. [s3]\n\nAn evaluation would need comparable evidence on completed capacity, dependable supply, network performance, and financing. Installed capacity alone would not answer every question about electricity available to households and businesses. This page therefore does not turn the plan’s targets into an observed annual series.",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "evaluating-results",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What would establish results?",
        "content": "A sound economic-policy account should distinguish budget authorization from actual expenditure, nominal changes from changes in purchasing power, and aggregate production from household outcomes. Annual comparisons also require consistent reporting periods: an Iranian reporting year spanning parts of two Gregorian years cannot silently become a Gregorian calendar-year observation.\n\nThe accessible evidence supports this introductory framework, but not a complete assessment of monetary, exchange-rate, subsidy, trade, or fiscal policy through the cutoff. Those subjects remain explicit research tasks. No current rules, rates, or forecasts are presented here as verified facts.",
